==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{confix|anthropo|sophy|lang=en}}, from a Renaissance Latin {{m|la|anthroposophia}} (attested in Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa, d. 1535, and Thomas Vaughan, d. 1666), popularized from the 1910s via German {{m|de|Anthroposophie}} ([[:w:Rudolf Steiner|Rudolf Steiner]], 1861–1925).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|/ˌænθɹəˈpɑsəfi/|lang=en}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˌænθɹəˈpɒsəfi/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# Human wisdom; knowledge or understanding of human nature.
# A spiritual movement inaugurated by Rudolf Steiner (also capitalized as [[Anthroposophy]]), postulating the existence of an objective, intellectually comprehensible spiritual world accessible to direct experience through inner development.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|anthroposophic}}
* {{l|en|anthroposophist}}

